              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\nThe \"Examine Insecure Code\" vulnerability in C# often arises from the use of the `unsafe` keyword, which allows for pointer manipulation and direct memory access. This can lead to security issues such as buffer overflows, memory corruption, and other vulnerabilities if not handled carefully. The `unsafe` code bypasses the type safety and memory safety features of C#, making it prone to errors that can be exploited by attackers.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Avoid Using Unsafe Code**: Whenever possible, avoid using `unsafe` code. Use safe alternatives provided by the .NET framework.\n2. **Code Review and Testing**: Conduct thorough code reviews and testing to ensure that any `unsafe` code is necessary and correctly implemented.\n3. **Limit Scope**: Restrict the use of `unsafe` code to the smallest possible scope.\n4. **Use Managed Code**: Prefer managed code constructs that provide safety and security.\n5. **Security Audits**: Regularly perform security audits to identify and mitigate potential vulnerabilities.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIf you must use `unsafe` code, ensure that it is properly encapsulated and reviewed. Here's an example of how you might refactor unsafe code to a safer alternative:\n\n#### Unsafe Code Example\n\n```csharp\nunsafe\n{\n    int* p = null;\n    // Potentially dangerous operations\n}\n```\n\n#### Safer Alternative\n\nInstead of using pointers, use managed arrays or collections:\n\n```csharp\nint[] array = new int[10];\n// Safe operations on the array\n```\n\n### Library Dependencies\n\nThe example provided does not require any additional library dependencies beyond the standard .NET libraries.\n\n### Relevant Resources\n\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices - Quick Reference Guide](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer)](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n\nThese links were verified to be active and accessible to anonymous users."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS172412"
                ]
